  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1032 PM CST MON NOV 23 2026\n\nWe had a few batches of light rain lingering across the region\nthis evening as a cold front continues to work through the\nregion. Much drier air is already working into the region as\nevident by the 00Z KBMX sounding which recorded a PWAT of 0.95\",\ndown from 1.27\" on the 12Z flight Monday morning. GOES TPW\nimagery reveals PWATs down to 0.3\" across the northern part of\nthe state. This will allow for a chilly night with lows ranging\nfrom right around freezing across our northern counties to the\nmid to upper 40s further south. Despite a weak ridging building\nin on Tuesday, temps will remain on the cooler side as a stout\nCAD wedge helps keep an east-northeasterly wind in place.\n\nIf we take a look at water vapor imagery, we can see our next\nupper trough digging over the western US. This trough is still\nexpected to be working south through the Plains, becoming a\ncutoff low somewhere over east Texas. Ahead of this closed low,\nflow aloft will switch back to the SW, allowing for quick\nmoisture returns in the upper-levels. At the surface however,\nwinds will remain from the E/NE, with relatively dry conditions.\nGiven the stout forcing with this closed low, most of this upper-\nlevel moisture should still find its way to the surface in the\nform of rain. With that said, recent forecast soundings do hint\nat a steady moistening of the lower levels through the day\nWednesday which suggests we could see a few periods of heavy\nrainfall. At this time, we are not expecting any strong to severe\nstorms with this system. Most locations can expect to see between\n0.5-1.5\" of rainfall through Wednesday night. These rainfall\ntotals may increase in the coming days, depending on how deep\nthat moisture return is ahead of this system. Rain looks to\nfinally come to an end late Wednesday, leaving most of\nThanksgiving dry. However, we will carry some low chances across\nour northern tier of counties to account for wrap-around\nmoisture.\n\nA cold front moving through on Thanksgiving will bring much drier\nair to the region with PWATs falling to around 0.20\". This dry\nair and a northerly flow will allow overnight lows to drop into\nthe mid 20s to low 30s Friday and Saturday morning. Southerly\nflow quickly resumes on Saturday, bumping temps up several\ndegrees. Overall, pleasant weather for the late stages of the\nweek.\n\n95/Castillo",
